y B8 will not be removed! I will keep it even if D8. So I pick **(B, 8)**. This is the optimal move.\n\n\n", 'output': "Situation: I currently control the top-left region and possess two key corners (A1 and A8), giving me a significant structural advantage. The bottom rows (G, H) are largely empty, making moves there risky. My goal is to consolidate my territory and extend from my stable corners without opening up opportunities for the opponent in the empty regions.\n\nEvaluation: \n- (B, 5) is a safe internal move that fills a gap and captures pieces, but it doesn't improve my structural control on the edges.\n- (F, 4) fills a gap in the center and flips opponent pieces, but might invite play into the empty bottom rows.\n- (B, 8) is a move on the right edge, immediately adjacent to my secure corner at A8.\n- Moves in the G row (G2-G6) are risky as they project influence into empty space, likely giving the opponent easy counter-attacks to claim the bottom edge or corners.\n\nReason: (B, 8) is the strongest move because it extends from my established corner at A8. By placing a piece at B8, I create a permanently stable disc that the opponent can never flip (as it is protected by the corner). Furthermore, this prevents the opponent from playing at B8 themselves, which would otherwise allow them to establish a foothold on the edge and threaten my corner's security. Securing the edge connected to a corner is a fundamental winning strategy in Reversi.\n\nAnswer: (B, 8)"}
