ur pieces (A8-D8) anchored by a corner. It is a "quiet" move (flips few pieces), which is excellent for preserving mobility. It also blocks the opponent from taking this spot.\n2.  **Move (D, 7)**: This is an internal move that flips pieces diagonally. However, playing here might leave D8 open for the opponent to play later, potentially challenging my edge control.\n3.  **Move (E, 6)**: This move flips a large number of pieces on row E. While it gains material, flipping too many internal pieces early can limit future move options and give the opponent more surfaces to play against.\n4.  **Moves on Row G**: These moves extend into the empty "frontier" next to the bottom edge. Playing here is risky as it often allows the opponent to slip behind and take the bottom edge or corners.\n\nReason: Move (D, 8) is strategically superior because it extends a stable group of pieces from a corner I already possess (A8). It denies the opponent a foothold on the right edge and does not open up any dangerous lines for them. It is a low-risk, high-reward positional move.\n\nAnswer: (D, 8)'}
