es a lot of openings for the other player. This move might leave the other side with few moves, which is a good thing to consider.\n\nAfter a few moves, the options narrow. The other moves (**G2, G3, G4, G5, G6**), are generally risky, as they open my position to attack at the edge of the board. I'm focusing on preserving mobility and not giving the opponent easy captures.\n\nI'm thinking about **A7** – I wonder if it’s a valid move, since it's an edge move. Unfortunately, it isn't.\n\nNow, as I'm thinking about the options and the endgame is coming, parity becomes extremely important, as the player that makes the last move is most likely to win. I will be looking for a quiet move.\n\nI then go on to analyze all of the above moves as a sequence to see what move is the most advantageous to play given the potential moves the other player could do afterwards, starting by the G moves and then the F moves.\n\nAmong all of the moves, the best is **(F,6)**, as it doesn't give the other player options and it will force them to play a certain way, like on E6, where I would have an advantageous position. This move looks to restrict the opponent, maintain board control, and gives a good setup for the final phase of the game.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the late mid-game phase with 23 empty squares. I control the top and left edges (rows A and B mostly, column 1). The opponent occupies the center. The bottom rows (G, H) are completely empty, and the right edge is partially open. My goal is to restrict the opponent\'s mobility, forcing them into bad moves, while preserving my stable discs and parity.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Moves like (G, 2) through (G, 6) extend into the empty G-row, which would likely give the opponent access to the corners or the H-row, so these are risky.\n- (F, 4) fills a hole but flips 7 discs in multiple directions, creating a large \'Black\' wall that might give the opponent pivot points to access the G-row (e.g., at G5).\n- (E, 6) is a "loud